Subject: [PATCH] =?UTF-8?q?raster:=20H6=20=E2=80=94=20the=20coherence=20la?=
 =?UTF-8?q?yer's=20resident=20surfaces=20and=20the=20one-wait=20draw?=
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

the sample of the real game said the draw_guest round-trip carried a
third of the main thread: the per-draw texture and plane allocations,
the full decode loops on both sides, and three waits a draw. the H6
residency lands the shape the Vulkan raster's surfaces already have:

- the color target mounts as a resident surface (check_surface_at),
  keyed with its rasterization scale, texture at the scaled dims, the
  shadow still holding the guest's own bytes — a draw stages only what
  the guest changed since the last fence
- the depth twin is a resident float image with its readback planes and
  its own shadow; upload_depth/download_depth (per-draw waits and
  allocations) are gone, the CPU staging and the fence decode remain
- one command buffer a draw: the staged depth blits, the render pass,
  the readback blits and the Managed sync ride one submission — one
  wait a draw three waits were
- sync_guest takes the memory now: the fences write the resident images
  back before they drop, guest memory canonical at every fence point
- the honest dispatch: every refusal (state, packs, binds) leaves the
  arm undrawn — nothing claims drawn what the host did not draw, the
  software path lands those pixels instead

ALBW with --rasterizer hardware --renderer metal runs past the 30-45 s
freeze (was: the write-through's per-draw round trip), audio produces
at the Vulkan rate, 90 s alive. 156 gpu tests green, the fill fence's
own surface drops and the frame's other residents legitimately stay.

the next look at the meter: batch draws into one buffer (the ring
spans are already per-submission) and shrink the refusal volume — the
software fills still carry the frame's mass.
